# Sprite effects
@scenario tutorial_lesson_18
@include /definitions/characters.vns
@label start
[mode dialogue standard]
[set ui.dialogueUi project]
[bg room]
[char ginger global on]
[set ui.dialogueFadeMs 160]
[stage clear]
[show ginger beside_code thoughtful]
[set tutorial_source scripts/tutorial/18_plugins_and_line_boil.vns]
[set tutorial_excerpt on_effect]
[set ui.dialogueFacet tutorial_code]
ginger: A sprite effect can change the rendered outline without requiring another pose. Compare the same image with the line-boil effect enabled and disabled.
[if !tutorial_still]
# region on_effect
[set character.ginger.imageEffect pikupiku.lineboil]
# endregion
ginger: The active effect is {b}pikupiku.lineboil{/b}. Look at the sleeve outline for a moment before advancing.
[else]
ginger: Reduced motion is enabled, so the example skips the animated effect. The same sprite stays visible.
[endif]
[set tutorial_excerpt off_effect]
# region off_effect
[set character.ginger.imageEffect none]
# endregion
ginger: The script sets character.ginger.imageEffect to {b}none{/b} here. Compare the outline with the preceding line.
ginger: If both look the same with motion enabled, check that the Pikupiku plugin is available. Keep the pose and lighting fixed while testing.
ginger: An expression selects the source drawing. An image effect changes how that drawing is rendered. Compare the sleeve against the plain background so small outline changes are easy to see.
ginger: The effect setting belongs to the character and lasts until another command changes it. Clearing it explicitly gives the following section a known baseline.
[clear ui.dialogueFacet]
> Isolate a sprite effect -> practice
> Review question -> check
> Back to topics -> return_hub
@label check
ginger: What makes a useful baseline for judging this effect?
> A different pose and new lighting. -> retry
> The same pose with its effect off. -> correct
> Back to topics -> return_hub
@label retry
[show ginger guide confused]
ginger: Changing the pose or lighting also changes the outline. Isolate the image effect for this comparison.
[jump check]
@label correct
[show ginger guide normal]
ginger: Use the same sprite, pose and lighting with the effect disabled, then enable it and compare.
> Back to topics -> return_hub
> Replay example -> start
@label return_hub
[clear ui.dialogueFacet]
[if !tutorial_done_18]
[flag tutorial_done_18]
[inc tutorial_count]
[endif]
[stage clear]
[goto TutorialHub:topics_page_3]
[end]

@label practice
[show ginger beside_code thoughtful]
[set tutorial_source scripts/tutorial/18_plugins_and_line_boil.vns]
[set tutorial_excerpt off_effect]
[set ui.dialogueFacet tutorial_code]
ginger: Capture the same expression with the effect off and on. Keep the character position, lighting, scale and window size fixed so the outline is the only moving part.
ginger: Look at several frames with movement enabled. A single frame can look ordinary even while a sequence shows an irregular outline. Then compare that sequence with the still route.
ginger: If the outline never changes, check the effect identifier, the targeted character and whether the plugin is available in the actual runtime you launched.
ginger: Leave the effect enabled across an extra line, then move the reset above that line. Watch which line loses the effect. That shows the lifetime of the setting.
@label practice_question
ginger: The following lesson should start clean. Where should the example reset the effect?
> Only before the first time it is enabled. -> practice_hint
> On every exit route that could leave it enabled. -> practice_result
> Back to topics -> return_hub
@label practice_hint
ginger: A reset at the beginning cannot undo a setting applied later. Follow the state along the route out of the example.
[jump practice_question]
@label practice_result
ginger: A reset at the exit restores a predictable state for the next section. Try leaving immediately after the effect line as well as finishing normally.
[clear ui.dialogueFacet]
> Review question -> check
> Try the exercise again -> practice
> Back to topics -> return_hub
